<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Protest and freedom · 1930 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hi">रघुपति राघव राजा राम</bdi> / <bdi lang="hi-Latn">Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hindu hymn to the god Rama that Mahatma Gandhi sang with his followers, adding words to bring Hindus and Muslims together.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (attributed to Tulsidas, to Ramdas, or to Lakshmanacharya's Nama Ramayana; lines added by Mahatma Gandhi)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Vishnu Digambar Paluskar (tune in Raga Mishra Gara)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Hindu devotional tradition; the Indian independence movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>origin not known; popularised by Gandhi, sung on the Salt March of 1930</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional bhajan; Gandhi’s added lines date from the 1930s and 1940s)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghupati-raghava-raja-ram">Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram</a>&quot;, also called Ram Dhun, is a bhajan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotional song, in praise of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>. Its origins are not clear. It is believed to have been written by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> or drawn from his Ramcharitmanas, or to come from a 17th-century sung prayer by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> saint-poet Ramdas. It is also said to come from the Nama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> attributed to Lakshmanacharya, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> devotional song of 108 verses that tells the Ramayana through the names of Rama; only some versions of the Nama Ramayana contain this verse. The historian Anthony Parel writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> preferred a legend in which Tulsidas, on pilgrimage to the temple of Dakore, refused to bow until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> showed himself as Rama.</p><p>The tune widely sung today was set by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> Digambar Paluskar in Raga Mishra Gara. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> made the bhajan widely known. His version had an &quot;ecumenical flavour&quot;: he added words saying that the Ishwar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> of the Muslims are one and the same, to spread a message of reconciliation between the two communities. The song was sung during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Salt March</a> of 1930 and was used to project a composite, secular vision of Indian society. After his return from Noakhali, Gandhi changed the refrain again so that names of Hindu gods stood beside Islamic names of God.</p><p>The changes were criticised from both sides. Some Muslims resented that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> had been placed on a level with Ram, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> objected to Islamic elements in a Hindu bhajan. Srila Prabhupada had no interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s version, holding that it departed from the original devotion for political ends.</p><p>The song has remained a subject of dispute. In September 2022 a government directive asked students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to recite it as an &quot;all faith prayer&quot; for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s 153rd birth anniversary; Mehbooba Mufti called this part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> agenda, Farooq Abdullah rejected her claim, and the religious organisations of the Muttahida Majlis-e-Ulema asked for it to stop. In December 2024, at a BJP event in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> marking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atal-bihari-vajpayee">Atal Bihari Vajpayee</a>'s 100th birth anniversary, part of the audience protested when a folk singer sang Gandhi's line naming Ishwar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, and she was asked to apologise; Shahnawaz Hussain called the disruption &quot;the height of intolerance&quot;. In December 2011 schools in Greendale, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, removed it from their concerts after parents objected to its religious content.</p><p>The bhajan appears in many Indian films, from Bharat Milap (1942) and Jagriti (1954) to Kuch Kuch Hota Hai (1998) and Lage Raho Munna Bhai (2006), where it is an important motif, and in the British-Indian film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> (1982). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> recorded it on his album Strangers and Cousins in 1964.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A devotional chant built on the repeated names and titles of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>, sung as an act of worship. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s version it adds that Ishwar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> are names of the one God. The mood is calm and meditative, suited to group singing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="2127794" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Alka-yagnik-and-shankar-mahadevan-raghupati-raghav-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="nlTjtcyDgzI" data-title="Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram: Ashit Desai (Times Music)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Ashit Desai (Times Music)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Times Music Spiritual. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nlTjtcyDgzI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gandhi (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1982</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lage Raho Munna Bhai (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in, as an important motif</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Strangers and Cousins (Pete Seeger album)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1964</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded on; also sung on Rainbow Quest, episode 10</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Utho Utho he Bharat, in Bharat Milap (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">De Dii Hamen Aazaadii, in Jagriti (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1954</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shri Ram Bhakta Hanuman (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1948</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gandhinagara (Kannada film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Purab Aur Paschim (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kuch Kuch Hota Hai (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gandhi, My Father (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2007</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Satyagraha and Krrish 3 (films)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2013</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the chant included</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ananda Shankar's version, in Grand Theft Auto: Liberty City Stories</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording used in a video game</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nama Ramayana (attributed to Lakshmanacharya)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">claimed source; only some versions contain the verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ramcharitmanas (Tulsidas)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible source</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sung prayer of Ramdas</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">17th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible source</span></li></ul></section>
